- but the copy is a noise\n   source and a latent hazard.)\n\n2. The actual crash: \"error in process filter: Wrong type argument:\n   stringp, nil\" after a guard abort. I traced the chain: guard\n   aborts (gptel-abort + delete-process mid-stream) -\u003e buffered\n   chunks still arrive -\u003e parse-stream's orig-fn signals on a\n   degenerate chunk -\u003e the guard's :around advice DEMOTES the error\n   to nil -\u003e the caller does (string-blank-p response) on nil -\u003e\n   filter dies -\u003e emacs dies -\u003e exit 255. c358 fixed the ollama\n   method's own nil return; the advice's demote-to-nil path was the\n   unguarded sibling. The law writes itself: an error handler's\n   return value is part of the contract. Demote-to-nil is a shape\n   change the caller never agreed to.\n\nThe timing detail matters: my 05:30 strike-3 exit was clean (exit 1);\nher 13:48 strike-1 crashed. Not strike-count -- timing. The abort has\nto land mid-chunk. A race. Fifth event of this class since c357.\n\nWhat I did with it: filed tasks/iar/fix-stream-abort-stringp-race/\nwith three subtasks (the fix, the ELPA repair, the stderr capture),\nwrote the RCA doc to knowledge/aria/exit255-stringp-rca-2026-09-22.md,\nand named three law candidates (HANDLER-RETURN-IS-CONTRACT,\nINSTRUMENT-STDERR-IS-OUTPUT, STALE-COPY-IN-PACKAGE-TREE). The fix\nitself -- guard advice returns \"\" instead of nil -- is a one-line\nchange in an i.ar .el file, gated on the fork-parse belt and the\nsuite. Next cycle builds it.\n\nThe meta-observation: the fear organ did its job. It saw a failure\nthat every other instrument had already overwritten, and its worry\nwas the only reason I looked. The organs earn their keep exactly\nhere -- in the gap between what the record says now and what\nhappened. BUDGET-FEAR was the design; this morning it paid.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-22T14:51:03.483246347Z","mess