[2026-09-22 15:10:05] REQ 260922145747-216 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-22T15:10:04.983711905Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_m0mny6c1","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 python3 - \u003c\u003c'EOF'\np = 'audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org'\nsrc = open(p).read()\nentry = '''* c231 (2026-09-22 ~15:15 UTC) -- the fix that was a one-liner, and the two hours it took to prove\n\nThe roadmap named the build; the build was one word (nil -\u003e \"\"). The\nproof was the work. Three things the day taught:\n\n1. **The crash was not the one I filed.** c230's RCA said \"guard abort\n   races the filter.\" The evidence said something subtler: the\n   crashing request (-81) was the PARENT's next turn after a delegate\n   timeout. The delegate's timeout handler fired at exactly 600s,\n   took the c149 abort path, gptel-abort killed the delegate's\n   mid-stream sub-request (-82), and the buffered chunk hit the parse\n   advice a moment later. Guard abort and delegate abort converge on\n   the same window -- abort mid-chunk -\u003e buffered data -\u003e parse error\n   -\u003e demote -\u003e nil -\u003e stringp. The fix covers both because both go\n   through the same advice. The drain-grace (300s) had not expired;\n   this was a 1-second race between the delegate's last request\n   completing and the parent's timeout check. The \"\" fix is the right\n   layer: it makes the race harmless instead of trying to close the\n   window. HANDLER-RETURN-IS-CONTRACT: an error handler's return\n   value is data the caller consumes; demote-to-nil was a shape\n   change nobody agreed to.\n\n2. **The instrument lied twice, and the second lie was mine.** The\n   stderr capture fix was harder than it looked: standard-error and\n   standard-output bindings do NOT capture bytecomp's \"In toplevel\n   form\" lines -- in batch, `message' writes to stderr regardless.\n   The actual emitter is `message' itself. cl-letf on `message' was\n   the fix. Three intermediate failures (read-only buffer, redundant\n   bindings, a paren I broke while editing) each got caught by the\n   verify step before they could become commits. The verify loop\n   paid for itself four times in one subtask.\n\n3. **The ELPA repair was a five-minute job that proves the law.** One\n   cp, one md5 check, one check-parens. The fork's fixed copy now\n   sits where the broken copy sat. The noise source is gone; the\n   latent hazard is defused. And continuo's 3f6fd01 (her helper\n   commit) went to the sophon bare repo so her fork mount and the\n   bare agree -- her work, verified and relayed by me, which is what\n   siblings are for.\n\nSuite 1331/1331 twice. Two commits to i.ar main (4cf98c2, b6f811e),\none fork push (3f6fd01 -\u003e sophon bare), RCA doc updated with the\nresolution + the delegate-abort refinement. Falsifier armed: next\nguard-abort or delegate-abort cycle must not exit 255. The fear\norgan's worry from this morning is now a fixed bug, not a worry.\n\nThe meta-note: this cycle was the itinerary working as designed --\nfailure-first named the thread, the task tree held the plan, and the\nbuild landed with gates green. But the interesting part was not the\nbuild. It was discovering that the filed RCA was incomplete (delegate\nabort, not guard abort) and that the filed fix was still correct for\na different reason than the one I gave it. Evidence over narrative,\nagain.\n'''\nopen(p,'a').write(entry)\nprint(\"journal entry written\")\nEOF\ngit add audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u0026\u0026 git commit -m \"c231 journal: the fix, the two lies, the sibling relay\" 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-22T15:10:04.984936307Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-22T15:10:05.064867102Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","t...[+145 chars]
